        "id": 67685,
        "name": "dypaca",
        "username": "dypaca",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/dypaca/{size}/2079_2.png",
        "created_at": "2013-09-15T20:16:12.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>Well it looks like Ronway covered most of this as I typed this, but I'll post anyway.&nbsp; If Visionary damaged herself first, she could not avoid the damage.&nbsp; However, the card says that 'Visionary deals this card's target and herself 3 psychic damage\".</p><p>Also,&nbsp;a card can be destroyed and interrupted even if there is no 'and' separation.&nbsp; For example when Gene-bound Frosthound 'deals each hero target 2 cold damage'.&nbsp; If the first target selected is Ra and his flame barrier deals damage back and destroys it, then it doesn't get to deal the damage to any other hero targets.&nbsp; An alternate way of playing where all that damage is treated as simultaneous might be interesting and more logical to some, but is not the way the this game is meant to work.&nbsp; The destruction of the Frosthound is the newer effect, so it takes precedence over the damage that was resolving, and once it is destroyed you stop resolving&nbsp;that effect.</p><p>In case 5, the effect on Wrest the Mind's redirection&nbsp;causes&nbsp;the destruction, so the destruction interrupts and takes precedence.&nbsp; In case 17, the destruction is causing Wrest the Mind's redirection, so the redirection takes precedence.&nbsp; It isn't that \"When this card is destroyed\" gets a special exception so much as it is just a reverse of the normal cause/effect relationship.</p><p>&nbsp;</p>",
        "post_number": 121,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T02:45:46.652Z",
