      {
        "id": 67686,
        "name": "Envisioner",
        "username": "Envisioner",
        "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/e/9de053/{size}.png",
        "created_at": "2013-09-15T21:17:00.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"Ronway\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/ronway/48/1856_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Ronway:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Considering the text on the cards are also the rules, I would say the fact that a card has a destruction effect written on it already says it functions differently than a card that does not have one. If it functioned the same, than it wouldn't have a destruction effect.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>The rules aren't on the cards.&nbsp; The rules are in the rulebook; the cards can contradict the rules, but only do so where they specifically do so.&nbsp; If you want to suspend a rule on a card, you have to do so explicitly.&nbsp; In the case of Sonic Mine, you'd do that with something like \"If Sonic Mine would be destroyed, first it deals 2 damage to each non-hero target, then it is destroyed.\"&nbsp; (Technically you should also specify \"This ability does not replace itself\", but that much can generally be left implied, as it's pretty obvious.)</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Now Citizen Hammer will do all hero targets 3 fire damage, however you do the damage 1 at a time in game terms.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That is certainly not how all games handle simultaneous multi-target damage, and it's an interpretation which leads to a lot of potential problems, which is probably why most games don't do it that way.&nbsp; I can think of no other examples where something that should hit the entire board at once instead pauses to resolve triggered events after every single target gets hit.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>It goes in order of the card, so Hammer will take the damage first.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That should really have been written as \"Visionary deals that target 3 damage, then deals herself 3 damage\".&nbsp; Compressing it into a single clause makes it sound like it should all happen at once and be impossible to interrupt.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Hammer recieves his 3 psychic damage, thus causes him to be reduced to 0. Since Hammer is now at 0 HP, he is effectively destroyed and is leaving play. Oh look, Wrest the Mind has a trigger for if the target it is attached to leaves play</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>But why would it be triggered by \"effectively\" rather than \"actually\"?&nbsp; If all cards are put into the trash instantly upon receiving lethal damage, then Sonic Mine can't work.&nbsp; Therefore, if Sonic Mine does work, cards should not be put instantly into the trash, but should be destroyed whenever an effect isn't being resolved that requires them to still be in play to have an effect.&nbsp; (Magic the Gathering solves this very simply by saying a card <em>doesn't</em> have to be in play for its effect to resolve, if it was triggered under appropriate circumstances; the effect stays in motion, like an arrow in flight, even if the \"bow\" which launched it has disappeared, until the effect reaches the \"target\" and is done \"flying\".)</p>\n<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"dypaca\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/dypaca/48/2079_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> dypaca:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>In case 5, the effect on Wrest the Mind's redirection&nbsp;causes&nbsp;the destruction, so the destruction interrupts and takes precedence.&nbsp; In case 17, the destruction is causing Wrest the Mind's redirection, so the redirection takes precedence.&nbsp; It isn't that \"When this card is destroyed\" gets a special exception so much as it is just a reverse of the normal cause/effect relationship.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>That at least seems to make some sense.&nbsp; But I still think the two rulings are inconsistent with each other, and that ideally the wording should be changed.</p>",
        "post_number": 122,
        "post_type": 1,
