      {
        "id": 67687,
        "name": "Ronway",
        "username": "Ronway",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/ronway/{size}/1856_2.png",
        "created_at": "2013-09-15T21:31:35.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"Envisioner\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/e/9de053/48.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Envisioner:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>In the case of Sonic Mine, you'd do that with something like \"If Sonic Mine would be destroyed, first it deals 2 damage to each non-hero target, then it is destroyed.\"&nbsp;</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<div>\nI've already suggested the wording being changed previously, just to clear up any confusion that can be caused by it.\n<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"Envisioner\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/e/9de053/48.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Envisioner:</div>\n<blockquote>\n</blockquote></aside></div>\nThat is certainly not how all games handle simultaneous multi-target damage, and it's an interpretation which leads to a lot of potential problems, which is probably why most games don't do it that way.&nbsp; I can think of no other examples where something that should hit the entire board at once instead pauses to resolve triggered events after every single target gets hit.\n\n\n<p>This isn't all other games however, this is Sentinels of the Multiverse: The Card Game.</p>\n<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"Envisioner\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/e/9de053/48.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Envisioner:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>That should really have been written as \"Visionary deals that target 3 damage, then deals herself 3 damage\".&nbsp; Compressing it into a single clause makes it sound like it should all happen at once and be impossible to interrupt.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>Things don't truly happen at once when Sentinels of the Multiverse: The Card Game's mechanics are concerned.</p>\n<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"Envisioner\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/e/9de053/48.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Envisioner:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>But why would it be triggered by \"effectively\" rather than \"actually\"?&nbsp;</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>Word it however way you want.</p>\n<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"Envisioner\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/e/9de053/48.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Envisioner:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>If all cards are put into the trash instantly upon receiving lethal damage, then Sonic Mine can't work.&nbsp; Therefore, if Sonic Mine does work, cards should not be put instantly into the trash, but should be destroyed whenever an effect isn't being resolved that requires them to still be in play to have an effect.&nbsp;</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>Sonic Mine does work, simple and easy as that. Does it need al ittle adjusting to the wording? Sure it does, but until than we will have to know how it is intended to work and play that way.</p>\n<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"Envisioner\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/e/9de053/48.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Envisioner:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Magic the Gathering solves this very simply by saying a card <em>doesn't</em> have to be in play for its effect to resolve, if it was triggered under appropriate circumstances; the effect stays in motion, like an arrow in flight, even if the \"bow\" which launched it has disappeared, until the effect reaches the \"target\" and is done \"flying\".</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>This is Sentinels of the Multiverse: The Card Game, not Magic the Gathering.</p>",
